id: rule-array-read-no-bounds
title: Without the bounds
topic: Arrays
example-of: ARRAY-READ
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule ARRAY-READ to decide whether this triple can be derived.

given:
  Assignment expression: $a[i]$

pre: a[i] > 0
post: x > 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  x := a[i]      // r
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The substitution
rule: ARRAY-READ
focus: r
text:
  As with the rule ASSIGN, we substitute the variable $x$ with the
  assignment expression $a[i]$ in $Q$.
calc:
  (x > 0)[x <- a[i]]     // Q is x > 0
  = a[i] > 0             // x replaced by a[i]
set: R1 = a[i] > 0

step: The read must be defined
rule: ARRAY-READ
focus: r
text:
  The expression reads the array at position $i$, so the rule adds
  $def(a[i])$: position $i$ must exist.
calc:
  def(a[i])
  = 0 <= i < sizeof(a)   // the positions of a are 0, ..., sizeof(a) - 1
set: R1 = a[i] > 0 && 0 <= i < sizeof(a)

step: P does not imply R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
  Nothing in $P$ says that position $i$ exists. Take the array $a$ with
  the values 4, 0, 9 and $i = 7$: position 7 does not exist, so the read
  $a[7]$ has no value. In Java it raises an exception, and in C its
  behaviour is undefined. The rule cannot derive the triple; adding
  $0 <= i < sizeof(a)$ to $P$ repairs it.
